**Priority**: ✅ COMPLETED

**Status**: This blocker has been resolved. `ErrorBoundary` is now a functional component using `react-error-boundary` (see `components/ErrorBoundary/index.tsx`). The `IErrorBoundaryProps` interface was renamed to `ErrorBoundaryProps`. All three usage sites (`AuthenticatedElement.tsx`, `WizardStep.tsx`, `WizardRoutedStep.tsx`) have been updated.

For reference, this is what was implemented:
```tsx
// react-error-boundary package approach (what was used)
import { ErrorBoundary as ReactErrorBoundary } from 'react-error-boundary';

const ErrorBoundary = ({ appInsights, children }: ErrorBoundaryProps) => (
    <ReactErrorBoundary
        FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}
        onError={(error, info) => {
            appInsights.getAppInsights().trackException({ error, severityLevel: SeverityLevel.Error, properties: { ...info } });
        }}
    >
        {children}
    </ReactErrorBoundary>
);
```

No more `getDerivedStateFromError` or `componentDidCatch`. The `onError` callback pattern receives the error as a parameter directly.

**Priority**: ✅ COMPLETED

**Status**: This blocker has been resolved. `WizardForm/index.tsx` now uses `React.Children.forEach` with an accumulator array. The implementation is the first migration approach listed below.

For reference, what was implemented (lines 27-33):
```tsx
const reactChildren: React.ReactElement<WizardStepProps<FormikValues>>[] = [];
React.Children.forEach(children, (child) => {
    if (!React.isValidElement<WizardStepProps<FormikValues>>(child)) {
        throw new Error('WizardForm only accepts children of type WizardStep');
    }
    reactChildren.push(child);
});
```

The runtime type guard is preserved — invalid children still throw immediately. The `React.Children.toArray` Legacy API is no longer in use. Key stability is no longer affected by child key prefixing.

**Priority**: ✅ COMPLETED

**Status**: Completed. `env.ts` now uses `declare global { var ... }` for all 17 config vars + `globalThis.*` for access + a `requiredVars.forEach` startup guard that calls `console.error` for missing vars.

For reference, the pattern that was implemented:
```ts
declare global {
    var REACT_APP_B2C_CLIENTID: string | undefined;
    var REACT_APP_B2C_AUTHORITY: string | undefined;
    // ... all 11 vars
}

export const env: EnvType = {
    REACT_APP_B2C_CLIENTID: globalThis.REACT_APP_B2C_CLIENTID ?? '',
    // ...
};

const requiredVars: (keyof EnvType)[] = [
    'REACT_APP_B2C_CLIENTID', 'REACT_APP_B2C_AUTHORITY', ...
];
requiredVars.forEach((key) => {
    if (!globalThis[key]) console.error(`[env] Missing required runtime variable: ${key}`);
});
```

This eliminates the `(<any>window)` casts, provides typed `globalThis` access, and gives a fast startup error instead of mysterious auth/telemetry failures.
